{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_50","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,120p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Implementation notes\n\n## Changes\n\n- Added a 16-entry, direct-mapped table of 2-bit saturating branch counters\n  in `rtl/core.sv`, indexed by PC bits `[5:2]` and reset to weakly not taken.\n- `rtl/if_stage.sv` recognizes legal conditional branch encodings, forms the\n  B-type target from the fetched instruction, and advances fetch using the\n  BHT prediction. A predicted-taken bit travels through IF/ID and ID/EX;\n  EX reconstructs the predicted next PC from the saved branch PC and immediate.\n- `rtl/ex_stage.sv` compares the resolved architectural next PC with the\n  carried prediction. Redirect and flush happen only when they differ; the\n  resolved branch direction trains the table when EX advances the branch.\n- The existing JAL/JALR path is preserved by comparing its resolved target\n  against the sequential prediction. Misaligned control transfers retain the\n  existing trap behavior and sequential architectural next PC.\n\n## Deviations and concerns\n\n- The table is implemented in `core.sv` rather than as a separate module so\n  the existing test source lists do not need a new RTL source entry. The\n  pipeline carries one direction bit and reconstructs the predicted next PC\n  in EX, avoiding a 32-bit ID/EX metadata register.\n- Indexing only by the low four word-address bits allows aliasing between\n  branches. There is no target cache; taken targets come directly from the\n  fetched branch immediate.\n- Gowin timing option 0 reports 5.165 MHz, 12,935 logic cells, and 493\n  registers. The worst path is still the existing MEM/WB forwarding through\n  EX and the ALU; Fmax is below the 5.442 MHz baseline figure recorded in\n  `core.yaml`, so the IPC gain must outweigh this timing/area regression.\n- The exact lint command using `cores/bench/rtl/*.sv` puts `core.sv` before\n  `core_pkg.sv` and fails on package typedef ordering. The repository's\n  `make lint TARGET=bench` compiles the package first and passes.\n\n## Local checks\n\n- Lint: pass with `make lint TARGET=bench`.\n- Formal: pass, 53 checks passed and 0 failed. Invoked with\n  `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench bash formal/run_all.sh` so the\n  script can select the single-retire wrapper from `core.yaml`.\n- Cosim: pass for selftest and CoreMark (`elfs_tested: 2`) with\n  `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench`. The default manual `make cosim` build\n  uses NRET=2 unless overridden, which does not match this core's NRET=1\n  interface.\n- Gowin timing: pass, place option 0 completed with the figures above.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_51","type":"agent_message","text":"Implemented the 16-entry, weakly-not-taken BHT. Fetch predicts legal conditional branches from their immediates; EX redirects only when the resolved next PC differs. The pipeline carries one prediction bit and reconstructs the predicted PC in EX.\n\n**Checks:** `make lint TARGET=bench` passed; formal passed (53/53); `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench` passed for selftest and CoreMark.\n\nGowin timing completed at **5.165 MHz**, below the recorded 5.442 MHz baseline. The critical path remains the existing forwarding-to-ALU path, so the IPC gain needs to offset the timing and area cost. Details are in [implementation_notes.md](/srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6-luna_xhigh-v2-rep2/cores/bench/worktrees/hyp-20260929-003-r1s2/cores/bench/implementation_notes.md).\n\nThe exact glob-based lint command fails because it compiles `core.sv` before `core_pkg.sv`; the repository lint target orders the package first and passes."}}
